> Derive the score and distinguish the two pairings

For nonempty strings of byte lengths n and m, the retained greedy rule scans
first-string indices left to right. Each takes the leftmost unoccupied equal
second-string byte within

$$
w=\max(\lfloor\max(n,m)/2\rfloor-1,0).
$$
\alttext(The allowed byte displacement is the longer length divided by two, rounded down, minus one and clamped at zero.)

Let M be the number of accepted partners. Mark both matched index sets, traverse
each in its own increasing index order, and let h be their number of unequal
byte comparisons. When M>0,

$$
t=h/2,\qquad J=\frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{M}{n}+\frac{M}{m}+\frac{M-t}{M}\right).
$$
\alttext(Jaro averages the two matched fractions and a matched-order agreement term using half the disagreement count.)

A disagreement count need not be even: ABCDEF versus BCADEF has six matches and
three order disagreements, so t=1.5 and J=11/12. This t is an algebraic
half-disagreement count, not a minimum number of adjacent edits or swaps. For
MARTHA/MARHTA, M=6 and h=2 give J=17/18. All three terms are in [0,1], so the
base score is bounded. With no accepted partners the score is zero; equal bytes
occurring outside the windows do not contribute. Both empty strings are assigned
one, and exactly one empty string is assigned zero by convention.

With capped common byte-prefix length L<=4 and finite scale p in [0,0.25], the
library uses the unconditional refinement

$$
W=J+Lp(1-J)=(1-Lp)J+Lp.
$$
\alttext(The unconditional prefix refinement is a convex combination of the Jaro score and one for the accepted scale domain.)

This proves J<=W<=1 because0<=Lp<=1. At the default p=0.1 the MAR prefix adds
1/60, yielding W=173/180. There is no boost-threshold parameter in this API;
other implementations may withhold the bonus below a base-score threshold. At
p=0.25, sharing four bytes forces W=1 even for different suffixes, so the upper
endpoint of the supported scale domain can collapse unequal strings. Invalid,
infinite and NaN scales now throw before allocation instead of producing
unbounded or nonfinite values. Default and other valid-scale arithmetic is
retained.

> Retain all five original pairs

These are the exact original spellings, order and cases. The two measures
coincide for the disjoint pair because it has no matched byte or common prefix.
The plot uses actual single-pass results; the numeric pair index refers to the
table rather than implying a calibrated relationship between different pairs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jaro_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto pairs = dm::algorithms::jaro_winkler_name_pairs();
    std::vector<double> index, jaro, winkler;
    std::print("| pair index | first | second | matches | order disagreements | prefix | Jaro | Winkler " "|\n| ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < pairs.size(); ++i) {
        const auto & [a, b] = pairs[i];
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::jaro_winkler_run(a, b);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} |\n", i,
                   a, b, r.matches, r.order_mismatches, r.prefix, r.jaro,
                   r.winkler);
        index.push_back(double(i));
        jaro.push_back(r.jaro);
        winkler.push_back(r.winkler);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(index, jaro, "Jaro", {35, 95, 210}, 9).points(index, winkler,
                                                           "Winkler",
                                                           {210, 55, 40}, 6).legend({"Jaro", "unconditional Winkler"}, {{35, 95, 210}, {210, 55, 40}}).title("All five original pairs: actual base and refined scores").x_label("pair index in the table").y_label("byte-string similarity").xlim(-.5, 4.5).ylim(-.04, 1.15).save_svg("build/jaro_original.svg");
}
